**Final review corrections**

- `tasks.md` now assigns
  `tests/ExtractionWorkflow.Tests/BoundaryTests.cs` narrowly to T04 for the two
  approved `CaseProcessing` and `ClientIntake` registrations. Every other
  boundary assertion remains unchanged.
- `IExtractionIngestWorkflow.GetDocumentAsync` and `GetCaseAsync`, plus the
  otherwise-unused `CaseExtractionView`, were removed because completed T04 code
  did not consume them. T06 may introduce the read contract it actually needs in
  the same pass that consumes it.
- The two-read path now compares the complete source bytes before extraction.
  A refused second read or changed content fails closed before the extraction
  provider, store append, or audit append. Both reads use the same four-part
  server-stored scope, every lease is disposed before return, and in-memory
  source copies are cleared while their leases remain live.
- `scripts/test-projects.json` records the final
  `ExtractionWorkflow.Tests` floor of 308.

**`[ASSUMED]` Class B decisions, for one-pass human override**

1. `IExtractionIngestWorkflow` declares only the members T04 implements.
   `architecture.md` sketches classification-override and duplicate-resolution
   on this interface, but T05 owns those policies and does not own this file;
   declaring them here would leave an unimplemented contract, which rule 2
   forbids. They belong on `IExtractionReviewWorkflow`.
2. `ExtractionWorkflowOptions.ProviderCallLimit` is the one configured value —
   a fail-closed bound on one provider call, never a quality signal, and not a
   confidence bound. No confidence bound exists anywhere in this feature.
3. Classification and extraction each take their own content lease because the
   provider consumes a stream once from its current position. The workflow keeps
   bounded in-memory copies only for the duration of the synchronous operation,
   compares them before extraction, clears them while the leases are live, and
   disposes both leases before returning.

**Gate unchanged.** T04 ships no HTTP route, no UI component, no registration in
the composition root, no migration, no production content reader, no production
document-intelligence provider, no correction or acceptance path, no review
record, and no deployment authorization. Each belongs to a later task.
